/target
# `crates/yantra-wasm` is excluded from the workspace (see Cargo.toml), so cargo gives it
# its own target dir. The root `/target` rule is anchored and does not reach it — and an
# untracked build directory inside the tree is not merely clutter: `gate.sh` fingerprints
# every untracked file, so it would invalidate the verdict cache on every wasm build.
# Belt to tools/build-sassembly-web.sh's braces. That script writes OUTSIDE the tree by
# default, because `tools/check-secrets-native.sh` reads untracked files and a base64 wasm module
# trips its AWS-token pattern — a generated demo inside the repo turns the gate red. This
# rule only matters if someone passes an in-tree path anyway.
/web/sassembly.html
**/*.rs.bk
.DS_Store
# third-party specification cache: 300+ MB, reproduce with research/specs/fetch-specs*.sh
research/specs/*/
research/specs/*.log

# third-party fonts: fetched, not committed (tools/fonts/fetch-fonts.sh pins the hash)
tools/fonts/*.ttf
tools/fonts/*.otf

# golden failures leave these next to the expectation for inspection
tests/golden/*.actual.*
tests/golden/*.diff.*

# isolated build dir used by the weight enforcer
/target/weight-check

# runtime log of the headless loop agent (tools/loop-agent.sh); the durable
# record of a cycle is .loop/log/YYYY-MM.tsv, not this
.loop/agent.log
.loop/agent.lock/
# launchd redirects the agent stdout/stderr here; the cycle record is agent.log
.loop/agent.stdout.log
.loop/agent.stderr.log

# FastBuild offline coordination: machine-local agent holds, rewritten on
# every `fastbuild working`. Local state, not repository content.
lockrule.md

# The keepalive daemon's pid, also written by `fastbuild working` — and until
# 2026-08-17 the one artifact of it that was NOT ignored (`W-066`).
#
# That made the supervision starve the rail it supervises. A rail check begins
# with `fastbuild working` on every run without exception; the daemon dropped
# this file; the loop's PROTOCOL §1 dirty-tree guard then skipped every fire
# afterwards. Four consecutive ticks were lost to it — 20:14, 20:19, 20:24,
# 20:29 — and the log said "working tree is dirty" each time, which was true and
# named a file no cycle had written and no human had touched.
#
# A pid is machine-local by definition; it means nothing in another checkout.
.fastbuild-keepalive.pid

# `adopt` writes this marker; `fastbuild working` writes the pid above. W-066
# ignored the pid and did not look for its sibling, which is fixing the instance
# and not the class — the same mistake, one file apart.
#
# I then made it worse: `git add -A` COMMITTED this file in 61f6ea2 while I was
# arguing it should not be in the repo. Machine-local state, pushed. Untracked
# here and kept on disk, because it is load-bearing right now for a reason that
# is NOT this project's to fix (see `W-070`): a duplicate project resolves ahead
# of the real one, so deleting the marker would silently point every command at
# an empty project.
.fastbuild.json

# Generated by `tree-sitter generate` from grammar/tree-sitter-sassembly/grammar.js
# (task B-034b). Not committed: `tools/check-grammar.sh` regenerates it before
# every check, so a committed copy could drift from the grammar it claims to be
# built from and nothing would notice — which is the failure mode this row was
# written to end.
grammar/*/src/

# The same, for T1 (`D-002h`, 2026-08-31). This grammar lives under `crates/`
# rather than `grammar/` and so was outside the rule above; without this line
# `tree-sitter generate` leaves an untracked `parser.c` that `tools/gate.sh`
# fingerprints and `tools/check-secrets-native.sh` reads. Not committed for the reason
# the T0 entry gives: a committed copy could drift from the grammar it claims
# to be built from and nothing would notice.
crates/tree-sitter-t1/src/

# The headless agent's stderr, overwritten each cycle (W-042). Machine-local
# diagnosis, not history: the cycle log records what happened.
.loop/agent-stderr.log

# The headless agent's own debug trace, overwritten each cycle (W-043).
# The `*` is load-bearing: the runner ROTATES this file, and `.loop/agent-debug.log.1`
# was a 10 MB untracked file the rule did not reach. `tools/gate.sh` fingerprints every
# untracked file and `tools/check-secrets-native.sh` reads them, so a rotation silently invalidated
# the verdict cache and put a debug log in front of the secret scanner (F-002e2a).
.loop/agent-debug.log*

# The driver-divergence escalation counter (`8cccb4de`), written beside the lock
# so a STALE driver tree is reported louder the longer it stays stale.
#
# UNTIL 2026-09-18 IT WAS NOT IGNORED, AND IT STOPPED THE RAIL COMPLETELY. The
# loop's PROTOCOL §1 dirty-tree guard refuses to run while `git status` is
# non-empty; this file made it non-empty; and THE ONLY THING THAT WOULD CLEAR IT
# IS THE RAIL. Twenty consecutive fires were lost from 13:49:34Z, every one
# logging `dirty=1 file(s): .loop/driver-diverged.count` — true, and naming a
# file no cycle had written and no human had touched.
#
# THAT IS `W-066` AGAIN, WORD FOR WORD, fourteen lines above: the keepalive pid
# on 2026-08-17 starved the rail it supervised in exactly this way. The ignore
# list is correct for the runtime files that existed when each entry was added,
# and nothing makes it notice a new one — so the SECOND writer of runtime state
# into a guarded tree reproduces the first writer's outage. Any file a loop drops
# beside its lock belongs here in the same commit that creates it.
.loop/driver-diverged.count

# Symlink the agent CLI creates beside --debug-file (W-043).
.loop/latest

# Every Bash command a cycle runs, logged before it runs (W-050). Machine-local.
.loop/bash-commands.log

# The gate verdict, keyed to a tree fingerprint (W-059). Machine-local.
.loop/gate-result
baselines/editor-electron/

# Agent worktrees. `.claude/worktrees/` is where subagent fleets are given
# isolated checkouts, and on 2026-08-29 twenty of them broke the gate: the
# `secrets` step walked one worktree's downloaded test262 fixture, found an
# `.npmrc`, and failed the whole run after 700s. Repo-wide checks must not see
# scratch trees that are not the repo (`W-143`).
.claude/worktrees/
.loop/tick-agent*.log

# launchd writes these live — ai.paramtatv.sansos.deep-gate.plist redirects the
# daemon`s stdout/stderr straight into the worktree every hour. Tracked, they made
# the trunk perpetually dirty and forced a `grep -v deep-gate` workaround into
# tools/tick-agent.sh:88 and :149 that the other six callers of
# `git status --porcelain` never got. Daemon output is not source.
.loop/deep-gate.stdout.log
.loop/deep-gate.stderr.log

# ── THE PROPERTY, NOT THE LIST (2026-09-08) ───────────────────────────
# EVERY `.log` DIRECTLY UNDER `.loop/` IS RUNTIME OUTPUT AND IS NOT SOURCE.
#
# The reason was written once, at `.loop/agent.log` above: "the durable record
# of a cycle is .loop/log/YYYY-MM.tsv, not this." It is true of every runtime
# sink in this directory — and it was then applied ONE FILENAME AT A TIME, as
# each new one bit somebody: agent.log, agent.stdout.log, agent.stderr.log,
# agent-stderr.log, agent-debug.log*, bash-commands.log, tick-agent*.log.
# Thirteen entries, spread from line 31 to line 108, one property.
#
# The fourteenth was missed. `ai.paramtatv.sansos.deep-gate.plist` redirects a
# launchd daemon's stdout and stderr into this directory hourly, those two
# paths were TRACKED, and a gitignore rule does not apply to a tracked file —
# so the trunk was perpetually dirty and a `grep -v 'deep-gate'` workaround was
# written into tools/tick-agent.sh:88 and :149 that the other six callers of
# `git status --porcelain` never received.
#
# A LIST DRIFTS FROM ITS PROPERTY SILENTLY: nothing goes red, no margin becomes
# false, and the file reads as though the case were handled — because for
# thirteen instances it was. The entries above are kept for their margins and
# are now subsumed; THIS is the mechanism. `.loop/log/` is a DIRECTORY of .tsv
# and is untouched by these patterns, and no tracked file under `.loop/` ends
# in `.log` (checked). The second pattern covers rotation (`agent-debug.log.1`).
.loop/*.log
.loop/*.log.*

# Python bytecode from tools/*.py — the storage model and probe.
__pycache__/

# JupyterLab LSP working copies of notebooks/ — regenerated on open, not source.
# Left untracked they enter the light gate scope and turn it RED on a markdown edit.
.virtual_documents/
.ipynb_checkpoints/

# ── LOCAL SECRET MATERIAL ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Credentials, tokens and keys for this machine only. Nothing here is ever
# committed, and the folder is deliberately absent from a fresh clone.
#
# THE LEADING SLASH IS LOAD-BEARING. A bare `secrets/` would also match
# `tools/secrets/` — the SCANNER's own crate — and ignore its entire source.
# This rule is anchored to the repository root and matches only the root folder.
#
# IGNORING IT IS NOT MERELY TIDINESS, for the two reasons this file already
# gives elsewhere: `tools/gate.sh` FINGERPRINTS every untracked file, so a
# rotated credential would invalidate the verdict cache on every gate run; and
# `tools/check-secrets-native.sh` READS untracked files, so an actual token sitting in an
# un-ignored folder would trip the scanner's own patterns and turn the gate RED.
# A base64 wasm module already did exactly that (see `/web/sassembly.html`
# above), and a rotated debug log did it again in `F-002e2a`. An ignored path is
# outside both walks.
#
# Ignored files are not reported by `git status --porcelain` and not listed by
# `git ls-files --others --exclude-standard`, which is what makes this work.
/secrets/

# GENERATED, AND NO LONGER TRACKED — owner ruling Q3, 2026-09-27 (`INFRA-003`).
# `.loop/DASHBOARD.md` is a rendering of `.loop/METRICS.tsv` and NOTHING reads it
# back: it is written at `metrics/src/main.rs` and read nowhere in the tree. Tracked,
# it was dirty by construction — `tick-agent.sh:49` records 72 fires and 72 refusals
# on 2026-09-20 over this file and its sibling, and both were the only paths that
# conflicted at every step of the 2026-09-27 trunk reconciliation.
#
# `.loop/METRICS.tsv` is deliberately STILL TRACKED: 886 of its 889 rows are
# carried forward by `--tree-only` because no tree walk can recompute them, so
# untracking it would discard the record on every fresh clone.
/.loop/DASHBOARD.md
